const e=[{id:"sv-3-216",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt compares mean **field-repair time (min)** for two contractors in two regions. The cell means behind the multi-vari chart are:

\`\`\`
Multi-Vari Chart for Repair_Time by Contractor - Region
Cell means
Region   Contractor   N    Mean
North    A           15    32.1
North    B           15    40.8
South    A           15    41.5
South    B           15    33.0

Contractor means:  A 36.8    B 36.9
Region means:      North 36.45    South 37.25
\`\`\`

Which interpretation is BEST supported?`,options:["There is a strong contractor × region interaction: which contractor is faster depends on the region, even though the overall contractor means are almost equal","Neither contractor nor region affects repair time, because the contractor means and the region means are almost identical","Contractor is the dominant source of variation, so contractor B should be replaced in both regions","Region is the dominant source of variation, so the South region process should be redesigned for both contractors"],answer:0,hint:"On a multi-vari chart, lines (or cell means) that cross show that the effect of one factor depends on the level of the other: an interaction.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A multi-vari chart can also show **two-way interactions**: when the connecting lines of one factor cross between the levels of another, the effect of the first factor changes with the second."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"In the North, A (32.1) is faster than B (40.8); in the South, B (33.0) is faster than A (41.5). The pattern reverses, so the lines cross. The marginal means (36.8 vs 36.9 and 36.45 vs 37.25) average the reversal away."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Equal marginal means do not mean 'no effect', and neither factor on its own explains the 9-minute gaps: the combination does. Next, ask what differs for A in the South and for B in the North, and confirm it with a test."}],final:"Crossing pattern = contractor × region interaction → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Looking only at the main-effect (marginal) means hides the crossing pattern; the cell means differ by about 9 min, so the factors do matter together.","Contractor B is slower only in the North; in the South it is about 8.5 min faster than A, so 'replace B everywhere' ignores the interaction.","The South is slow only for contractor A; for contractor B the South is faster than the North, so region alone is not the driver."],trap:"Equal main-effect averages can hide a large interaction: always read the cell means.",bookRef:"Book pp. 240–250"},{id:"sv-3-217",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A multi-vari study of server **inlet temperature (°C)** in a data hall measured the top, middle and bottom slots of two racks on two days:

\`\`\`
Multi-Vari: Inlet_Temp by Slot, Rack, Day
Day   Rack   Top    Middle   Bottom   Rack mean
Mon   R1     27.9   24.6     22.1     24.87
Mon   R2     28.3   24.9     22.0     25.07
Tue   R1     28.1   24.4     22.3     24.93
Tue   R2     28.0   24.8     21.9     24.90
Day means:   Mon 24.97    Tue 24.92
\`\`\`

Where should the team focus its search for Xs?`,options:["On rack-to-rack Xs, such as the power load of each rack, because the rack means differ","On day-to-day Xs, such as outside weather, because temperature always varies over time","On the thermometers, because a spread of about 6 °C inside one rack can only be measurement error","On positional (within-rack) Xs that differ between slot heights, such as airflow and hot-air recirculation at the top of the rack"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the spread inside each unit (positional), between unit means (cyclical) and between time-period means (temporal); chase the largest family.",steps:[{title:"Locate each family",body:"Within a rack (positional): top − bottom ≈ 28 − 22 = about **6 °C**. Rack to rack: rack means 24.87–25.07, about **0.2 °C**. Day to day: 24.97 vs 24.92, about **0.05 °C**."},{title:"Decide",body:"The positional family is by far the largest, so the Xs worth studying are the ones that change with slot height (cold-aisle airflow, missing blanking panels, recirculation of hot exhaust air)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The rack and day families are small, so Xs acting only on them can be set aside for now. A repeated, ordered pattern is a real process signal, not gauge noise."}],final:"Positional family dominates → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The rack means differ by only about 0.2 °C (24.87 to 25.07), a tiny family compared with the 6 °C spread from top to bottom.","The day means differ by only 0.05 °C (24.97 vs 24.92); the temporal family is the smallest here.","The top-to-bottom pattern repeats in the same order in every rack and on every day; random gauge error would not repeat like that.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 240–250"},{id:"sv-3-218",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt set up a multi-vari study of **chat handle time**: 3 shifts × 4 agents per shift × 5 chats per agent. In the worksheet she numbered the agents **1 to 12** across the three shifts. Minitab returns an error and will not draw the chart. What is the MOST likely fix?",options:["Label the agents generically 1–4 within each shift, so every shift has the same agent levels and the design is balanced","Add more chats for the agents with the longest handle times so that the variation is exposed","Remove the shift column, because a multi-vari chart can only show two factors","Transform handle time to make it normal, because multi-vari charts require normal data"],answer:0,hint:"Minitab's multi-vari chart needs a balanced design with generic labels inside each higher-level group.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book stresses that multi-vari data must be **balanced**, and units should be labelled **generically** inside each group (1–3 within each period, not 1–9)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Numbering agents 1–12 makes Minitab see 12 agent levels, each present in only one shift, so the design looks unbalanced. Relabel 1–4 within each shift (3 × 4 × 5 = 60 rows)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"More data for some agents, dropping a factor or transforming Y do not fix a labelling and balance problem."}],final:"Use generic 1–4 labels within each shift → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Adding data for some agents only makes the design more unbalanced; Minitab needs the same number of data points in every condition.","A multi-vari chart can show within, between and temporal families together; dropping shift throws away the temporal family.","A multi-vari chart is a graphical tool for patterns of variation and does not require normal data; the error is about the labels."],bookRef:"Book pp. 242, 247"},{id:"sv-3-219",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Two analysts draw multi-vari charts from the **same** data set on network latency, and their charts look quite different. What is the MOST likely reason?",options:["One of them used a sample too small to show 80% of the usual process variation","Multi-vari charts are random by nature, so each run gives a different picture","One analyst used data from a designed experiment instead of passive data","They entered the factors in a different order; the order changes how the chart looks, although the data are the same"],answer:3,hint:"The book: the look of a multi-vari chart depends on the order in which the factors are entered.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that **factor order matters**: the classic order is within, between, temporal, but you should use the order that shows the variation most clearly."},{title:"Apply",body:"Same data + different picture = different factor order (which factor is plotted inside, which on the axis, which as panels)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Sample size, randomness and study type are the same for both analysts because the data set is the same."}],final:"Different factor order → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Both used the same data set, so the amount of variation captured is identical; only the display differs.","A multi-vari chart is a fixed plot of the data: the same data and settings always give the same chart.","The stem says the data set is the same, so the way it was collected cannot differ between the two charts.",""],trap:"Try more than one factor order before deciding which family dominates.",bookRef:"Book pp. 248–250"},{id:"sv-3-220",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In a multi-vari chart of **call time** by centre, hour and agent, the 'centre' family looks largest. When the individual points are displayed, almost all of the centre difference comes from **one 95-minute call** in Centre 2; without it the centres overlap. What should the Black Belt do NEXT?",options:["Investigate that call as a possible special cause, and re-read the families with and without it before choosing where to focus","Conclude that Centre 2 is the root cause and start an improvement project there","Delete the call and report that the multi-vari study found no sources of variation","Replace the multi-vari chart with a Pareto chart of call reasons"],answer:0,hint:"Transactional data: show the individual points; one outlier can mislead the chart and usually has its own cause.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For transactional processes the book advises displaying the **individual data points**, because incoming work is not controlled and one outlier can mislead you. A single extreme outlier usually has its **own** cause."},{title:"Apply",body:"The 'centre' family is an artefact of one call. Understand that call (what made it last 95 minutes?) and re-read the chart with and without it."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Declaring Centre 2 the root cause or deleting the point both skip the investigation; a Pareto chart does not answer a variation-family question."}],final:"Investigate the outlier, then re-read the families → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","One extreme value is driving the apparent centre effect; a multi-vari chart never proves root cause, and here the signal is not even a family effect.","Deleting data without understanding it hides a possible special cause, and the other families still need to be read.","A Pareto chart shows which categories occur most often; it does not show which family of variation dominates, and it does not deal with the outlier."],bookRef:"Book pp. 248–250"},{id:"sv-3-221",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"While assigning the potential Xs from the **X-Y Matrix** to multi-vari families, the team finds that **agent experience** influences both within-shift and shift-to-shift variation. What does the book advise?",options:["Assign it only to the family with the larger spread and ignore the other","Assign the X to both families and split its weight between them with percentages","Drop the X from the study, because each X must belong to exactly one family","Run a separate multi-vari study for that X before plotting the main study"],answer:1,hint:"Before graphing, map each X to a family; an X that belongs to more than one family is split by percentage.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Before graphing, the book lists the potential Xs from the Measure-phase X-Y Matrix and assigns each X to a family. If an X belongs to more than one family, **split it with percentages**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Agent experience can be allocated, for example, 60% to within-shift and 40% to shift-to-shift, so whichever family turns out largest still points to it."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Picking one family, dropping the X or running an extra study are not what the book recommends."}],final:"Split the X across families by percentage → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Forcing the X into one family loses information about its contribution to the other family.","","Xs that act on several families are common; dropping them could remove a real cause.","A separate study is not needed; the book handles shared Xs by splitting them across families."],bookRef:"Book pp. 242, 247"},{id:"sv-3-222",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:1,stem:`A team lists four comparisons from a study of **optical power readings**:

| Row | Comparison |
|---|---|
| 1 | The 8 ports of one line card, read at the same moment |
| 2 | The averages of consecutive provisioning batches run back-to-back |
| 3 | Monday averages versus Thursday averages |
| 4 | The same port read three times in a row with the same meter |

Which row describes **between-unit (cyclical)** variation?`,options:["Row 1","Row 2","Row 3","Row 4"],answer:1,hint:"Within = positions inside one unit; between = consecutive units or batches; temporal = time periods.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's three classic families: **within-unit** (positional), **between-unit** (cyclical: consecutive pieces, groups or batches) and **temporal** (shift, day, week)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Consecutive batches are consecutive units → between-unit (cyclical)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Row 1 is positional, row 3 is temporal and row 4 is measurement repeatability."}],final:"Consecutive batches = cyclical → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Ports of one card read at one moment are positions inside one unit: within-unit (positional) variation.","","Comparing days is variation over time: temporal variation.","Repeated readings of the same port with one meter measure repeatability (measurement error), not a family of process variation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 240–250"},{id:"sv-3-223",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"When planning the data collection for a multi-vari study in Analyze, all of the following are good practice EXCEPT:",options:["Designing the sampling plan so that it captures within-unit, between-unit and temporal variation","Collecting the same number of readings for every combination of factors","Deliberately changing process settings between periods so that each family shows up more clearly","Collecting for long enough to show at least about 80% of the usual process variation"],answer:2,hint:"In Analyze a multi-vari study is passive: watch the process as it normally runs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At this point in DMAIC a multi-vari study is **passive**: you gather data without changing any factor."},{title:"Apply",body:"Changing settings on purpose turns the study into an experiment, and the families no longer reflect normal running."},{title:"Check the others",body:"A plan covering all three families, balanced data and at least 80% of the usual variation are all part of the book's method."}],final:"Changing settings on purpose is NOT passive → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is good practice: the book's first step is a sampling plan that covers within, between and temporal variation.","This is good practice: Minitab needs balanced data (equal data points for every condition).","","This is good practice: the book's guideline is that the data should show at least 80% of the usual process variation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 240–250"},{id:"sv-3-224",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A multi-vari study can help a team **rule out** groups of Xs: if a family of variation turns out to be small, the Xs that act only on that family are unlikely to be the main cause. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Multi-vari: find the largest family, and set aside the families that show little variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the goal of a multi-vari study is to learn what the cause **is and what it is not**."},{title:"Apply",body:"If a family's variation is small, Xs acting only on it cannot explain much of Y, so they drop down the list. The statement is true."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book says the goal is to learn what the cause **is and what it is not**; small families let the team set their Xs aside."],bookRef:"Book pp. 248–250"},{id:"sv-3-225",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A balanced multi-vari plan for **ticket triage time** samples **4 days**, **3 agents** per day and **6 tickets** per agent. How many rows of data will the Minitab worksheet contain?",options:["13","18","24","72"],answer:3,hint:"Rows in a balanced multi-vari plan = the product of the number of levels in each family.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Temporal: 4 days. Between: 3 agents per day. Within: 6 tickets per agent."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{rows}=\\text{days}\\times\\text{agents per day}\\times\\text{tickets per agent}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{rows}=4\\times 3\\times 6=72$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Every combination holds the same 6 readings, so the design is balanced, as Minitab requires."}],final:"72 rows → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Added the levels (4 + 3 + 6) instead of multiplying them.","Multiplied agents by tickets (3 × 6) and forgot the 4 days.","Multiplied days by tickets (4 × 6) and forgot the 3 agents per day.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 242, 247"},{id:"sv-3-226",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A multi-vari chart has just been drawn. Put these follow-up steps in the order the book recommends:

1. Confirm the suspected X with an appropriate hypothesis test
2. Identify the largest family of variation on the chart
3. List the Xs that act on that family
4. Carry the verified X forward into Improve

Which order is correct?`,options:["3 → 2 → 1 → 4","2 → 1 → 3 → 4","2 → 3 → 1 → 4","2 → 3 → 4 → 1"],answer:2,hint:"Find the family → list its Xs → verify with a test → act.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multi-vari narrows the search but does not prove cause. The book's next steps: focus on the Xs of the largest family and confirm them with the right hypothesis test."},{title:"Order",body:"Largest family (2) → Xs in that family (3) → hypothesis test (1) → verified X to Improve (4)."}],final:"2 → 3 → 1 → 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["You cannot know which Xs to list until you know which family is largest.","A hypothesis test needs a specific suspect X; list the Xs of the dominant family before testing.","","An X is carried into Improve only after it has been verified; skipping the test treats a graphical hint as proof."],bookRef:"Book pp. 248–250"},{id:"sv-3-227",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Descriptive statistics for **days to resolve a billing dispute**:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Resolve_Days
N     Mean    StDev   Median   Skewness   Kurtosis
180   12.8    7.9     9.6      2.14       6.02

Anderson-Darling:  A-Squared 6.87   P-Value < 0.005
\`\`\`

Which description of the shape is BEST supported?`,options:["Left (negatively) skewed, because the median is smaller than the mean","Platykurtic: a flat distribution with short tails","Approximately normal, because N = 180 is large enough for the central limit theorem","Right (positively) skewed: a long tail toward longer resolution times pulls the mean above the median"],answer:3,hint:"Skewness > 0 and mean > median → right skew; the longest tail points in the direction of the skew.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The skewness coefficient is about 0 for symmetric data, **positive for right skew** and negative for left skew. The mean is pulled toward the long tail."},{title:"Apply",body:"Skewness = 2.14 (> 0) and mean 12.8 > median 9.6, so the long tail is on the right, which is typical of a time with a natural lower limit of zero."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Left skew reverses the sign; positive kurtosis means peaked, not flat; and AD p < 0.005 means the data are not normal."}],final:"Right-skewed → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The tail points in the direction of the skew; a mean above the median and a positive skewness coefficient put the tail on the right, not the left.","Kurtosis is positive (6.02), which means a peaked shape with long tails (leptokurtic), not a flat one.","The CLT is about the distribution of sample means; it does not make individual resolution times normal, and AD p < 0.005 rejects normality.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-228",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Descriptive statistics for **call-setup time (s)**:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Setup_s
N     Mean     StDev    Median   Skewness   Kurtosis
120   1.204    0.052    1.201    0.08       -0.11

Anderson-Darling:  A-Squared 0.29   P-Value 0.611
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is worded correctly?`,options:["There is no evidence of non-normality: the data are roughly symmetric (mean ≈ median, skewness ≈ 0), so they can be treated as normal","The data are proven to follow a normal distribution, because the p-value is above 0.05","The data are leptokurtic, because the kurtosis value is not exactly zero","The data are skewed, because the mean and the median are not identical"],answer:0,hint:"AD test: H₀ = the data are normal; p > α → fail to reject (treat as normal), never 'proven'.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a normal shape, mean ≈ median ≈ mode and skewness and kurtosis are close to 0. For the Anderson-Darling test, $H_0$: the data are normal."},{title:"Apply",body:"p = 0.611 > 0.05 → fail to reject $H_0$; skewness 0.08 and kurtosis −0.11 are near zero. Treat the data as normal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"'Proven normal' is the classic wording trap; tiny deviations from zero are not evidence of skew or kurtosis."}],final:"Fail to reject normality; treat as normal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A p-value above 0.05 means 'fail to reject normality'; a test never proves that data are normal.","Kurtosis −0.11 is essentially zero; leptokurtic would need a clearly positive value (a tall peak with long tails).","A 0.003 s gap between mean and median, with skewness 0.08, is negligible; real data are never exactly symmetric."],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-229",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A histogram of **daily average throughput** is unusually **flat** with short tails, and Minitab reports a **negative kurtosis**. According to the book, what is the MOST likely cause?",options:["Several distributions with very different variances are laid on top of each other, producing a tall, narrow peak","The process mean shifted several times during data collection, so several distributions with different means lie side by side","The measurement system has poor resolution, so the data fall on only a few values","A natural lower limit at zero is compressing one side of the data"],answer:1,hint:"Platykurtic = flat like a plate (negative kurtosis), often shifting means; leptokurtic = 'leaping' peak (positive kurtosis), often mixed variances.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: **platykurtic** (flat, short tails, negative kurtosis) often comes from **several means shifting over time**, which build a plateau; **leptokurtic** (peaked, long tails) comes from distributions with very different variances."},{title:"Apply",body:"Flat + negative kurtosis → platykurtic → look for a mean that moved during the data collection (a mixture to separate and fix)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other options describe leptokurtosis, granularity and skewness."}],final:"Shifting means → platykurtic → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Very different variances stacked together give a peaked shape with long tails (leptokurtic, positive kurtosis), the opposite of what is seen.","","Poor resolution gives granular, step-like data, which is a separate class of non-normality.","A natural limit produces skewness (a long tail on one side), not a flat top."],trap:"Lepto and platy are often swapped in distractors: lepto = tall peak, platy = flat plate.",bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-230",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A dot plot of **jitter (ms)** from a probe shows values only at 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5 and 4.0, stacked in columns. The shape looks symmetric, yet the Anderson-Darling test gives **p = 0.003**. What should the team check FIRST?",options:["Whether two probes with different means have been mixed, creating two modes","Whether the data should be transformed so that a parametric test can be run straight away","The resolution of the measurement system, for example by recording more decimal places and checking the number of distinct categories","Nothing: symmetric data are normal, so the Anderson-Darling result can be ignored"],answer:2,hint:"Granularity = few distinct values (steps); causes: measurement resolution or categorical data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Granular** data have only a few distinct values; they can look normal but are not continuous, so the normality test fails. The book's first cause is **measurement system resolution**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Readings only every 0.5 ms suggest the probe rounds too coarsely. Improve the resolution (more decimals) and test again."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"There are no separate peaks (not a mixture); transforming hides the cause; ignoring p = 0.003 is wrong."}],final:"Check measurement resolution → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A mixture would show separate peaks; here the problem is too few distinct values (steps), not two modes.","The book warns against rushing to transform data; fix the cause of the non-normality (here resolution) first.","","Granular data can look symmetric and still fail the normality test; the low p-value is a real warning."],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-231",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"The daily **% of calls answered within SLA** sits between 96% and 100% on most days, with a few days down near 80%. Values can never exceed 100%. How should this distribution be described?",options:["Right (positively) skewed, because most of the data are bunched at the high end","Bimodal, caused by a mixture of two call centres","Platykurtic, caused by the mean shifting during the month","Left (negatively) skewed, caused by a natural upper limit at 100%"],answer:3,hint:"The longest tail points in the direction of the skew; natural limits cause skewness.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **longest tail points in the direction of the skew**. A natural or artificial limit is one of the book's causes of skewness."},{title:"Apply",body:"The data pile up against the 100% ceiling and trail off toward 80%: the long tail is on the left → left (negative) skew from a natural limit."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Naming the skew after the bunch of data is the classic reversal trap; there is no second peak and no plateau."}],final:"Left-skewed due to the 100% limit → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Skew is named after the long tail, not after where the data bunch up; the tail runs toward the low values (left).","There is one main peak near 96–100% plus a tail, not two separate peaks.","Platykurtic means a flat plateau; here the data are bunched against a limit with a long tail.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-232",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"The book lists several causes of **skewness**. All of the following are causes of skewness EXCEPT:",options:["Poor resolution of the measurement system","Natural or artificial limits, such as a time that cannot be below zero","Mixtures of data from sources that should be the same","Non-random patterns over time, such as tool wear"],answer:0,hint:"Skewness causes: limits, mixtures, non-linear relationships, interactions, patterns over time. Resolution belongs to granularity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Skewness causes: natural limits, artificial limits (sorting), mixtures, non-linear relationships, interactions and non-random patterns over time."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Measurement resolution is the cause of **granularity**, a different class of non-normality."}],final:"Resolution → granularity, not skewness → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Limits are the book's first cause of skewness.","Mixtures (machine A and B, two interviewers) are a listed cause of skewness.","Non-random patterns across time are a listed cause of skewness."],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-233",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which reference distribution is used to judge a test that compares the **variances of two normal populations**?",options:["The t distribution","The F distribution","The Poisson distribution","The binomial distribution"],answer:1,hint:"Means → t (or Z); one variance → chi-square; two variances → F.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each test statistic has a reference distribution: t for means, χ² for one variance and for contingency tables, **F for the ratio of two variances** (and for ANOVA)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Two normal variances → F-test → F distribution."}],final:"F distribution → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The t distribution is used for tests and intervals on means when σ is unknown.","","The Poisson distribution models counts of defects or events per unit.","The binomial distribution models the number of defectives in n trials."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–292, 340"},{id:"sv-3-234",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, non-normal data are always a sign that something is wrong with the process. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Skewness is often natural; the other three classes usually flag a problem.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: **non-normal distributions are not bad**; they often carry more root-cause information than normal data."},{title:"Apply",body:"Skewness near a natural limit (for example time ≥ 0) is a natural state. Kurtosis, multiple modes and granularity usually signal a problem, but not every non-normal shape does. The statement is false."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book says non-normal distributions are not bad: skewness is often a natural state near a limit, and non-normal shapes carry root-cause clues.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-235",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"Page-load time is normally distributed with mean **200 ms** and standard deviation **20 ms**. What proportion of page loads take **longer than 240 ms**? (Φ(2.00) = 0.9772)",options:["0.0228","0.0250","0.0455","0.9772"],answer:0,hint:"$Z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$, then the upper-tail area is $1-\\Phi(Z)$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\mu=200$ ms, $\\sigma=20$ ms, $x=240$ ms; we need the area **above** x."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Z=\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$Z=\\dfrac{240-200}{20}=\\dfrac{40}{20}=2.00$$"},{title:"Read the tail",body:"$$P(X>240)=1-\\Phi(2.00)=1-0.9772=0.0228$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 2.3% of page loads exceed 240 ms."}],final:"0.0228 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Used the empirical rule (95% within ±2σ, so 2.5% per tail) instead of the Z table.","Counted both tails (beyond ±2σ) although only times above 240 ms were asked for.","Read the area below Z = 2, the wrong side of the curve."],bookRef:"Book pp. 255–264"},{id:"sv-3-236",phase:3,topic:"3.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"To estimate the latency customers experience, an engineer places the measurement probe on a switch **inside the data centre**, so it measures only the internal hop and not the customer's path. The readings are repeatable, and the sample is random and large. Which type of error affects this inference?",options:["Error in sampling","Bias in sampling","Lack of measurement validity","Error in measurement (repeatability)"],answer:2,hint:"Four error types: sampling error, sampling bias, measurement error, lack of measurement validity (measuring the wrong thing).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's four errors in inference: error in sampling, bias in sampling, error in measurement and **lack of measurement validity** (the measurement does not measure what it should, like a probe in the wrong slot)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A repeatable probe on the internal hop does not measure customer latency: a validity problem. A bigger sample would not help."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Selection is random (no bias), readings are repeatable (no measurement error) and the sample is large (small sampling error)."}],final:"Measuring the wrong thing = lack of validity → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Sampling error is the random 'luck of the draw'; here the sample is large and random, so it is small.","Sampling bias comes from how units are selected; the problem here is what the probe measures, not which readings are chosen.","","The stem says the readings are repeatable; the probe is consistent but in the wrong place."],bookRef:"Book pp. 267–279"},{id:"sv-3-237",phase:3,topic:"3.2.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`The book gives a 5-step approach to inferential statistics. Which order is correct?

1. What kind of data does that tool require?
2. What do you want to know?
3. How will you collect the data?
4. What tool will give you that information?
5. How confident are you with your data summaries?`,options:["4 → 2 → 1 → 3 → 5","2 → 4 → 3 → 1 → 5","2 → 1 → 4 → 3 → 5","2 → 4 → 1 → 3 → 5"],answer:3,hint:"Question → tool → data needed → collection → confidence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Inference starts from the question, picks a tool, lets the tool decide the data and the collection plan, and ends by judging confidence in the summaries."},{title:"Order",body:"2 (what to know) → 4 (tool) → 1 (data the tool needs) → 3 (collect) → 5 (confidence)."}],final:"2 → 4 → 1 → 3 → 5 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The question to be answered must come first; the tool is chosen to answer it.","The data a tool requires must be known before planning how to collect them.","The tool must be chosen before you can know what data it requires.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 267–279"},{id:"sv-3-238",phase:3,topic:"3.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`Descriptive statistics for **incident resolution time (h)**:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Resolution_h
N    Mean   SE Mean   StDev   Minimum   Q1     Median   Q3     Maximum
64   5.32   0.31      2.48    1.10      3.60   4.95     6.70   13.40
\`\`\`

Which value tells the team how much the **sample mean** would typically vary from one random sample of 64 to the next?`,options:["2.48 (StDev)","3.10 (Q3 − Q1)","4.95 (Median)","0.31 (SE Mean)"],answer:3,hint:"Standard error of the mean: $SE=\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The spread of sample means from sample to sample is the **standard error of the mean**, $SE=\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$."},{title:"Check it",body:"$$SE=\\dfrac{2.48}{\\sqrt{64}}=\\dfrac{2.48}{8}=0.31$$"},{title:"Eliminate",body:"StDev, IQR and median all describe individual values, not the sampling error of the mean."}],final:"SE Mean = 0.31 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["StDev describes the spread of individual resolution times, not the spread of sample means.","The interquartile range describes the middle 50% of individual values.","The median is a measure of the centre of the data, not of sampling error.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 267–279"},{id:"sv-3-239",phase:3,topic:"3.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A service manager reports: *'Our sample of 25 repairs shows the mean repair time is 42 minutes.'* Why is this statement, on its own, a weak basis for a decision?",options:["It is a point estimate with no interval, so it gives no idea of how far the true mean could be from 42 minutes because of sampling error","It uses the sample mean instead of the median, which is always required for time data","A sample of 25 is too small for any inference to be made","It should have reported the standard deviation instead of the mean"],answer:0,hint:"Look beyond the point estimate to the interval around it (estimate ± about 2 standard errors).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every sample statistic is an estimate and could be wrong. The book says to look beyond a single **point estimate** to the interval around it."},{title:"Apply",body:"Report 42 min with a confidence interval (about ±2 standard errors) so the reader can judge whether a later change is larger than sampling noise."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Mean vs median, n = 25 and the standard deviation are not the core issue."}],final:"Point estimate without an interval → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The mean is a valid estimate; the median is preferred for skewed data, but that is not the weakness here.","Inference is possible with n = 25 (for example a t-based confidence interval); the problem is that no measure of uncertainty is given.","The standard deviation describes individual values; what is missing is an interval for the mean."],bookRef:"Book pp. 268, 277"},{id:"sv-3-240",phase:3,topic:"3.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Every sample statistic (not only the mean, but also the median and the standard deviation) has its own sampling distribution if samples are drawn again and again. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Any statistic computed from repeated random samples varies from sample to sample.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each new random sample gives a slightly different value of any statistic; plotting those values gives its **sampling distribution**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book says this explicitly for means, medians and standard deviations, so the statement is true."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book states that every statistic (means, medians, standard deviations) has a sampling distribution."],bookRef:"Book pp. 267–279"},{id:"sv-3-241",phase:3,topic:"3.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"To estimate satisfaction among **all** broadband customers, the team e-mails a survey to a random sample drawn from the list of customers registered on the **mobile app** (about 40% of the customer base). Which statement BEST describes the problem?",options:["There is no problem, because the sample was drawn at random","The sampling frame is incomplete, so the sample cannot represent customers who do not use the app, however large it is","The sample is too small; doubling it would remove the problem","The survey should use cluster sampling instead of random sampling"],answer:1,hint:"Frame = the list you sample from; a good frame is complete and lists every element once.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **frame** is the identifiable list the sample is drawn from. A good frame is **complete**; if it misses part of the population, the sample cannot represent it."},{title:"Apply",body:"App users are only 40% of customers and may differ from the rest (more digital, younger). The result describes app users, not all customers."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Random draws, a bigger n or a different scheme cannot recover people who are not on the list."}],final:"Incomplete frame → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Random selection from an incomplete list still misses everyone who is not on the list.","","A bigger sample from the same frame repeats the same bias; size does not fix a frame problem.","Changing the scheme does not help while the list itself leaves out 60% of customers."],bookRef:"Book pp. 141, 268"},{id:"sv-3-242",phase:3,topic:"3.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt is preparing an attribute agreement study for **call-quality scoring**. She plans to pick 20 calls **at random** from yesterday's recordings, but almost all of yesterday's calls scored between 80 and 85. What is the BEST advice?",options:["Keep the random selection, because random sampling is always the best way to choose items","Increase the random sample to 200 calls from yesterday","Select calls on purpose so they cover the full range of quality, from clearly poor to clearly good, and present them to the appraisers in random order","Use only calls scoring 80–85, because that is where most real decisions are made"],answer:2,hint:"How to sample depends on the purpose: MSA items are chosen across the full process range, then measured in random order.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: how to sample depends on what you want to know. For **measurement system analysis**, items are **not** a random sample; they are selected across the full range of the process and measured in random order."},{title:"Apply",body:"Choose calls that span poor to good quality, including borderline ones, then shuffle the order for the appraisers."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Random or larger samples from one narrow day cannot test the full scale."}],final:"Select across the full range → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Random sampling is right for estimating population values, but an MSA needs items spread across the whole range so the system is tested at every level.","More calls from the same narrow band still do not test the scoring of clearly poor or clearly good calls.","","A narrow band hides how well the appraisers separate good calls from bad ones."],bookRef:"Book pp. 192, 268"},{id:"sv-3-243",phase:3,topic:"3.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team wants to run a capability study on **order-entry time** (continuous data) and has **12** readings so far. What does the book say about the sample?",options:["12 readings are enough if the data pass a normality test","Capability studies need at least 100 readings whatever the data type","At least 30 readings are needed only if the data are attribute","Collect at least 30 readings for continuous data; attribute data would need many more"],answer:3,hint:"Capability: a minimum of 30 continuous samples; attribute data need a lot more.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: capability on **continuous** data needs a **minimum of 30** samples; **attribute** data need a lot more."},{title:"Apply",body:"12 readings are too few; collect at least 30 over enough time to show the normal process variation."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Normality does not replace sample size; the 100 rule and the reversed rule are not in the book."}],final:"At least 30 continuous readings → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Passing a normality test does not make a sample of 12 adequate for a capability estimate.","100 is not the book's rule; the minimum for continuous capability is 30.","This reverses the rule: 30 is the minimum for continuous data, and attribute data need far more.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 291–292, 397"},{id:"sv-3-244",phase:3,topic:"3.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A power analysis shows that a 2-sample t-test would need **26** tickets per group. Because the data are skewed, the team will use a **non-parametric** test instead. Using the book's adjustment, how many tickets per group are needed?",options:["31","29","26","23"],answer:0,hint:"$n_{\\text{np}}=\\dfrac{n_{t}}{0.864}$, rounded up.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$n_{t}=26$ per group; a non-parametric test will be used."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$n_{\\text{np}}=\\dfrac{n_{t}}{0.864}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$n_{\\text{np}}=\\dfrac{26}{0.864}=30.09$$"},{title:"Round up and interpret",body:"Always round a sample size **up**: 31 tickets per group."}],final:"31 per group → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Used the ×1.1 rule for estimating a mean from non-normal data instead of the non-parametric adjustment.","Kept the t-test sample size; a non-parametric test is less efficient and needs more data.","Multiplied by 0.864 instead of dividing by it (22.5 → 23), which lowers the sample size."],bookRef:"Book pp. 291–292, 378"},{id:"sv-3-245",phase:3,topic:"3.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are likely to introduce **sampling bias** into a baseline of ticket resolution times EXCEPT:",options:["Drawing ticket IDs with a random-number generator from the complete list of tickets for the period","Letting team leaders choose 'typical' tickets for the sample","Including only tickets that have already been closed, leaving out tickets still open","Taking all tickets from the day shift because the night shift's data are harder to extract"],answer:0,hint:"Bias comes from non-random or convenience selection; random selection from a complete frame prevents it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Bias in sampling** is a systematic error from a non-random or convenience procedure, and it does not shrink with n."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"A random draw from the complete list is exactly how the book prevents bias."},{title:"Check the others",body:"Hand-picked 'typical' tickets, closed-only samples and one-shift samples are all biased."}],final:"Random draw from a complete frame is NOT a bias source → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Letting the process owner pick examples is a classic source of bias.","Closed tickets only leaves out the long-running open ones and biases the time downward.","Sampling one shift for convenience misrepresents the other shift."],bookRef:"Book pp. 268, 291–292"},{id:"sv-3-246",phase:3,topic:"3.2.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A team will draw a **stratified random sample** of customers from three segments (consumer, SME, enterprise). Put the steps in order:

1. Randomly select the required number of customers within each segment
2. Define the population and obtain a complete frame (customer list)
3. Split the frame into the three segments (strata)
4. Decide the total sample size and how many to take from each segment`,options:["3 → 2 → 4 → 1","2 → 3 → 4 → 1","2 → 4 → 1 → 3","2 → 3 → 1 → 4"],answer:1,hint:"Frame → strata → allocation → random selection within each stratum.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stratified sampling divides the population into groups that **differ** and samples randomly **inside every** group."},{title:"Order",body:"Complete frame (2) → split into strata (3) → set n and the allocation (4) → random selection within each stratum (1)."}],final:"2 → 3 → 4 → 1 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["You cannot split a frame into strata before you have a complete frame.","","Random selection within strata needs the strata to exist first, so splitting cannot come last.","The number to take from each segment must be decided before customers are selected."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.2.2; Book p. 141"},{id:"sv-3-247",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`An analyst repeatedly takes random samples of **25** delivery times and records each sample mean:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Sample_Means (n = 25 each)
Variable       N     Mean    StDev
Sample_Means   200   46.3    2.4
\`\`\`

Using the central limit theorem, what is the best estimate of the standard deviation of **individual** delivery times?`,options:["0.48","2.4","12.0","60.0"],answer:2,hint:"$\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$, so $\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}\\times\\sqrt{n}$.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The SD of the sample means is the standard error: $\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$."},{title:"Rearrange and substitute",body:"$$\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}\\times\\sqrt{n}=2.4\\times\\sqrt{25}=2.4\\times 5=12.0$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Individual delivery times vary about five times as much as the means of 25."}],final:"σ ≈ 12.0 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Divided the SD of the means by √n again instead of multiplying.","Treated the SD of the sample means as if it were the SD of individual values.","","Multiplied by n instead of by √n."],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-248",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Individual **repair times** are right-skewed, and an I-MR chart of single values gives frequent signals on the long tail that turn out to be ordinary. The team switches to an **X̄ chart with subgroups of 5** consecutive repairs. Why is the X̄ chart better suited here?",options:["Averaging removes the skewness from the individual repair times themselves","By the central limit theorem, subgroup averages are closer to normal than the individual values, so the normal-based control limits fit better","Subgroups of 5 make the process mean smaller, so fewer points exceed the UCL","An X̄ chart does not use control limits, so it cannot give false alarms"],answer:1,hint:"CLT: averages tend toward normal even when individual values are not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CLT says the distribution of **sample (subgroup) means** approaches normal whatever the shape of the individual data; X̄ charts rely on this."},{title:"Apply",body:"Averages of 5 skewed repair times are much less skewed, so the ±3 standard-error limits behave as intended."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The raw data stay skewed, the centre does not move, and X̄ charts do have limits."}],final:"Subgroup means are near-normal (CLT) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The individual values stay skewed; only the distribution of the averages becomes more normal.","","The centre of the distribution of means equals the process mean; averaging does not shift it.","An X̄ chart has control limits; they are simply based on the spread of the subgroup means."],bookRef:"Book pp. 274–279, 519"},{id:"sv-3-249",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team samples **400** chat sessions, all from **premium** customers, to estimate the mean chat time of **all** customers. A colleague says: *'n is far above 30, so by the central limit theorem the confidence interval is valid for all customers.'* What is the BEST response?",options:["The colleague is right: with n = 400 the CLT guarantees the interval covers the mean of all customers","The interval is invalid because chat times are not normally distributed","The CLT makes the sample mean approximately normal, but it does not remove bias; the interval describes premium customers, not all customers","The sample must be raised to 1,000 so that the CLT covers all customer types"],answer:2,hint:"The CLT concerns random sampling error and the shape of the means, not bias.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CLT gives the shape and spread of the distribution of sample means under **random** sampling. It does not remove **bias** or measurement error."},{title:"Apply",body:"All 400 sessions come from premium customers, so the interval estimates the premium mean only."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Normality is not the issue at n = 400, and more of the same biased sample does not help."}],final:"The CLT does not fix bias → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The CLT describes random sampling error only; a sample from one segment is biased whatever its size.","With n = 400 the CLT copes with non-normal individual data; the problem is who was sampled.","","More premium customers still leaves out the other customers; bias does not shrink with n."],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-250",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Individual **handle times** are strongly right-skewed. A team member argues: *'With 500 data points the central limit theorem makes our data normal, so we can calculate Cpk with the normal model on the individual values.'* What is wrong with this argument?",options:["Nothing is wrong; the CLT applies once n ≥ 30","The CLT applies only to attribute data","The CLT applies only when the population is already normal","The CLT applies to the distribution of averages; individual handle times stay skewed, so capability on individual values needs a model that fits their actual distribution"],answer:3,hint:"CLT: averages become normal; individual values keep their own shape.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CLT does **not** make individual data normal; a skewed process stays skewed. Only the averages become normal."},{title:"Apply",body:"Cpk on individual values estimates how many individual calls fall beyond the limits, so it must use their actual (skewed) distribution, for example a non-normal capability analysis."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other options misstate when, and to what, the CLT applies."}],final:"The CLT is about averages, not individuals → answer **D**",whyWrong:["n ≥ 30 makes the distribution of sample means approximately normal, not the individual values.","The CLT applies to means of any data type with a finite variance; it is not limited to attribute data.","The whole point of the CLT is that sample means become normal even when the population is not.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-251",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"If the **population itself is normally distributed**, for which sample sizes is the distribution of sample means normal?",options:["For any sample size, even n = 2","Only when n is 30 or more","Only when n is at least 100","Never exactly; it is only approximately normal for large n"],answer:0,hint:"Normal parent → means normal for every n; non-normal parent → approximately normal for n ≥ about 30.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the distribution of sample means is **normal when the parent population is normal**, and **approximately normal for n ≥ 30** when the parent is not normal."},{title:"Apply",body:"Here the parent is normal, so the means are normal for any n."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The 30 and 100 thresholds belong to non-normal populations."}],final:"Any n → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","n ≥ 30 is the guideline for non-normal parent populations; a normal parent needs no minimum.","No minimum applies when the population is normal.","Means of a normal population are exactly normal for every n."],bookRef:"Book pp. 274–276"},{id:"sv-3-252",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book often recommend about **30** samples for summary statistics such as the mean?",options:["At n = 30 the standard error becomes zero","Beyond about 30 the standard error falls only slowly, and the t distribution is almost the same as the Z distribution","At n = 30 the individual data become normally distributed","At n = 30 sampling bias disappears"],answer:1,hint:"$SE=\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ drops steeply and then flattens; t approaches Z.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's SE-versus-n curve drops steeply and then flattens; its rate of change is almost zero by about 30 samples. That is also where t at 0.975 approaches Z = 1.96."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"SE never reaches zero, individual values do not become normal, and bias is not reduced by n."}],final:"SE curve flattens and t ≈ Z near 30 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The standard error keeps shrinking as n grows; it never becomes zero for a finite sample.","","Individual values keep their own shape; only the distribution of means becomes normal.","Bias does not depend on sample size; only random sampling error shrinks with n."],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-253",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"As the sample size n increases, what happens to the **mean (centre)** of the distribution of sample means?",options:["It decreases toward zero","It moves toward the median of the population","It stays equal to the population mean μ","It increases, because larger samples include more extreme values"],answer:2,hint:"As n grows: the centre stays at μ, the spread shrinks, the shape becomes normal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's die simulation shows three things as n grows: the **centre stays the same**, the variation decreases and the shape becomes normal."},{title:"Apply",body:"The mean of the sample means equals μ for every n."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Only the spread (the standard error) falls; the centre does not shift."}],final:"Stays at μ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The book's wording that 'the mean decreases' is misleading: it is the spread of the means (the standard error) that shrinks, not their centre.","The centre of the sample-mean distribution is μ, not the population median (they differ for skewed data).","","Extreme values in larger samples average out; the centre does not rise."],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-254",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The central limit theorem can be used only when the population of individual values is normally distributed. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"CLT: means become normal regardless of the population's shape.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CLT says the distribution of sample means becomes normal **no matter what the shape of the population** as n grows."},{title:"Apply",body:"A normal population is not required (it only means the means are normal for any n). The statement is false."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The CLT's key message is that sample means approach a normal distribution whatever the shape of the population.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-255",phase:3,topic:"3.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Queue wait has μ = **50 s** and σ = **12 s**. Random samples of **36** calls are taken. Within what range will about **95%** of the sample means fall (μ ± 2 standard errors)?",options:["49.33 s to 50.67 s","48 s to 52 s","46 s to 54 s","26 s to 74 s"],answer:2,hint:"$SE=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$; about 95% of sample means lie within $\\mu\\pm 2\\,SE$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\mu=50$ s, $\\sigma=12$ s, $n=36$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$SE=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$SE=\\dfrac{12}{\\sqrt{36}}=\\dfrac{12}{6}=2$$"},{title:"Build the range",body:"$$\\mu\\pm 2\\,SE=50\\pm 2\\times 2=50\\pm 4$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 95% of sample means fall between 46 s and 54 s."}],final:"46 s to 54 s → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Divided σ by n (36) instead of by √n.","Used ±1 standard error instead of ±2.","","Used σ of individual calls instead of the standard error of the mean."],bookRef:"Book pp. 270–279"},{id:"sv-3-256",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the courtroom analogy for hypothesis testing, the null hypothesis is the defendant who is presumed innocent. If the evidence is not strong enough, what is the correct verdict and its statistical equivalent?",options:["'Innocent': accept H₀; the null hypothesis has been shown to be true","'Guilty': reject H₀, because the defendant could not prove innocence","'Retrial': the test result is invalid and must be repeated","'Not guilty': fail to reject H₀; innocence has not been proven"],answer:3,hint:"H₀ is on trial: only 'reject' (guilty) or 'fail to reject' (not guilty).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the null hypothesis is **on trial**. Not enough evidence means 'not guilty', never 'proved innocent'."},{title:"Apply",body:"Not guilty ↔ **fail to reject H₀**. Nothing has been proven about H₀ being true."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"'Accept H₀' is the exam's favourite wording trap; rejection needs strong evidence; a retrial is not part of the logic."}],final:"Not guilty = fail to reject H₀ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Weak evidence does not prove innocence; 'accept H₀' overstates what the data show.","The burden of proof lies with the evidence against H₀; a lack of evidence cannot lead to rejection.","A non-significant result is a valid outcome; it does not invalidate the test.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 286–288"},{id:"sv-3-257",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An operations manager asks whether mean **handle time** is **different** from the 6-minute target; she does not mind in which direction. The test will use α = 0.05. How should the alternative hypothesis and the rejection region be set up?",options:["Hₐ: μ ≠ 6; two-tailed, with 0.025 of α in each tail","Hₐ: μ > 6; one-tailed, with all 0.05 of α in the upper tail","Hₐ: μ ≠ 6; two-tailed, with 0.05 of α in each tail","Hₐ: μ = 6; two-tailed, with 0.025 of α in each tail"],answer:0,hint:"≠ in Hₐ → two-tailed, α split in half between the tails; < or > → one-tailed.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"'Different' with no direction → **Hₐ: μ ≠ 6**, a two-tailed test, with α **split between both tails**."},{title:"Apply",body:"With α = 0.05 each tail holds 0.025 (Z critical values ±1.96)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"One tail ignores half the question; 0.05 per tail doubles α; '=' in Hₐ is never correct."}],final:"Hₐ: μ ≠ 6, 0.025 per tail → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The manager cares about either direction; a one-tailed test would ignore a mean below 6.","In a two-tailed test α is split in half; 0.05 in each tail would make the total risk 0.10.","The equals sign belongs in H₀, never in the alternative hypothesis."],bookRef:"Book pp. 286–288, 290"},{id:"sv-3-258",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team tests whether a new call script **reduces** mean handle time.

\`\`\`
Two-Sample T-Test and CI: New_Script, Old_Script
μ₁: population mean of New_Script
μ₂: population mean of Old_Script
Difference: μ₁ - μ₂

Estimation for Difference
Difference   95% Upper Bound for Difference
  -0.84         -0.26

Test
Null hypothesis          H₀: μ₁ - μ₂ = 0
Alternative hypothesis   H₁: μ₁ - μ₂ < 0
T-Value   DF   P-Value
  -2.41   57     0.010
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, which practical conclusion is correct?`,options:["Reject H₀: there is sufficient evidence that the old script has a lower mean handle time than the new script","Reject H₀: there is sufficient evidence that the new script has a lower mean handle time than the old script","Fail to reject H₀: the T-Value is negative, so the new script is not better","Accept H₁ with 99% certainty that the new script is better, because p = 0.010"],answer:1,hint:"Read the μ₁ and μ₂ definitions and the direction of H₁; reject H₀ when p < α.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"μ₁ = New_Script, μ₂ = Old_Script; H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ < 0 means 'new is lower'. P-Value = 0.010."},{title:"Decide",body:"0.010 < 0.05 → **reject H₀**. The upper bound −0.26 is below 0, which agrees."},{title:"Translate",body:"Practical solution: the new script reduces mean handle time, by about 0.84 on average."}],final:"Reject H₀; the new script is lower → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Reversed the direction: H₁ is μ₁ − μ₂ < 0 with μ₁ = New_Script, so the new script is the lower one.","","The sign of T only shows the direction of the difference; p = 0.010 < 0.05 leads to rejecting H₀.","The p-value is not the probability that H₁ is true; the conclusion is 'reject H₀', with risk α of a false alarm."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–290"},{id:"sv-3-259",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Each of the following is a correctly written **null hypothesis** EXCEPT:",options:["H₀: μ = 30 min","H₀: σ₁² = σ₂²","H₀: μ₁ ≠ μ₂","H₀: p₁ = p₂"],answer:2,hint:"The null always states 'no difference' and contains the equals sign.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"H₀ is the default 'no difference' statement and always contains the **equals sign**; a claim of difference (≠, <, >) goes in Hₐ."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"'μ₁ ≠ μ₂' states a difference, so it is an alternative hypothesis."}],final:"μ₁ ≠ μ₂ belongs in Hₐ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A null of no difference from a target, with the equals sign, is correct.","Equal variances is a correct null for a variance test.","","Equal proportions is a correct null for a 2-proportion test."],bookRef:"Book pp. 286–288"},{id:"sv-3-260",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's Analyze roadmap, an X has just been found **statistically significant**. Before it moves on to Improve, which other checks must it pass?",options:["None: statistical significance is enough to move to Improve","It must be re-tested at α = 0.01 to make sure the result holds","Its data must be shown to follow a normal distribution","It must also be practically significant and shown to be a root cause"],answer:3,hint:"Three gates: statistically significant? practically significant? root cause?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's Analyze map ends with three checks: **statistically significant? practically significant? root cause?** Only an X that passes all three moves to Improve and Control."},{title:"Apply",body:"After statistical significance, confirm that the effect is large enough to matter and that the X is a cause, not just something that moves with Y."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other options add the wrong gate or none at all."}],final:"Practical significance + root cause → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Statistical significance alone can flag tiny or non-causal effects; the roadmap adds two more checks.","Changing α after the fact is not part of the roadmap and is poor practice.","Normality is an assumption checked before some tests, not a gate for moving an X to Improve.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 286–290"},{id:"sv-3-261",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these Analyze activities in the order shown in the book's hypothesis-testing process map:

1. Translate the practical theories into statistical hypotheses
2. Identify the vital few Xs carried over from Measure
3. Perform the statistical tests and state the practical conclusion
4. State practical theories about how each X affects Y`,options:["2 → 4 → 1 → 3","4 → 2 → 1 → 3","2 → 1 → 4 → 3","2 → 4 → 3 → 1"],answer:0,hint:"Xs → practical theory → statistical hypotheses → test → practical conclusion.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's map: vital few Xs identified → practical theories → scientific (statistical) hypotheses → select tools → collect data → perform tests → practical conclusion."},{title:"Order",body:"2 → 4 → 1 → 3."}],final:"2 → 4 → 1 → 3 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The theories are about the vital few Xs, so the Xs must be identified first.","A statistical hypothesis translates a practical theory, so the theory must exist first.","The practical conclusion comes from the test, which needs the hypotheses written first."],bookRef:"Book pp. 286–290"},{id:"sv-3-262",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A team rejects H₀ at α = 0.05 with p = 0.02. Which statement is MOST accurate?",options:["There is sufficient evidence for Hₐ at the 5% significance level; the decision could still be wrong (a Type I error), so nothing has been proven","Hₐ has been proven true","There is a 98% probability that Hₐ is true","There is a 2% probability that H₀ is true"],answer:0,hint:"A decision about H₀ proves nothing; p = P(data this extreme | H₀ true).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's pitfalls: the decision is about H₀, and **nothing has been proved**; every decision can be wrong."},{title:"Apply",body:"p = 0.02 < 0.05 → reject H₀: sufficient evidence for Hₐ at α = 0.05, with a risk of a false alarm."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"'Proven', '98% probability Hₐ is true' and '2% probability H₀ is true' all misread the p-value."}],final:"Sufficient evidence, not proof → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A hypothesis test is a decision with a known risk, not a proof.","1 − p is not the probability that Hₐ is true; p is calculated assuming H₀ is true.","The p-value is the probability of data this extreme if H₀ were true, not the probability that H₀ is true."],bookRef:"Book pp. 286–290"},{id:"sv-3-263",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A new knowledge-base search should cut **ticket handling time**. The sponsor says a reduction of **2.0 min** or more would be worth the cost. A small pilot gives:

\`\`\`
Two-Sample T-Test and CI: Old_Search, New_Search
Sample       N   Mean   StDev   SE Mean
Old_Search   6   14.2   3.9     1.6
New_Search   6   10.7   3.6     1.5

Difference = μ (Old_Search) - μ (New_Search)
Estimate for difference:  3.50
95% CI for difference:  (-1.40, 8.40)
T-Test of difference = 0 (vs ≠): T-Value = 1.62  P-Value = 0.138  DF = 9
\`\`\`

What is the BEST conclusion?`,options:["Accept H₀: the two search tools have the same mean handling time","Fail to reject H₀: the observed 3.5-min reduction would matter, but the pilot is too small to show it is real; check the power and collect a larger sample","The reduction is statistically significant, because the 3.5-min estimate is larger than the 2.0-min target","Implement the new search, because the result is practically significant"],answer:1,hint:"p > α → fail to reject; a promising difference with a small n and a wide CI → low power, collect more data.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"P-Value = 0.138; the 95% CI for the difference is (−1.40, 8.40), which includes 0."},{title:"Decide",body:"0.138 > 0.05 → **fail to reject H₀**: not statistically significant."},{title:"Interpret",body:"The estimate (3.5 min) exceeds the 2.0-min practical threshold, but with n = 6 per group the test has little power. Size a larger study before deciding."}],final:"Not proven yet: low power, collect more data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["'Accept H₀' is the wrong wording, and the wide CI (−1.40 to 8.40) shows the data fit both no change and a large gain.","","Statistical significance comes from the p-value (0.138 > 0.05), not from comparing the estimate with the business target.","Practical value is not enough; the difference has not been shown to be more than chance."],trap:"Practically significant but not statistically significant usually means the sample was too small.",bookRef:"Book pp. 284–285, 290"},{id:"sv-3-264",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team must detect a difference of **0.7 standard deviations** in mean resolution time with **90% power** (α = 0.05). Minitab gives:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
1-Sample t Test
Testing mean = null (versus ≠ null)
α = 0.05   Assumed standard deviation = 1

Sample Size   Power   Difference
    10         0.9      1.15
    15         0.9      0.90
    20         0.9      0.76
    30         0.9      0.61
    40         0.9      0.53
\`\`\`

What is the smallest sample size in the table that meets the requirement?`,options:["15","20","30","40"],answer:2,hint:"The Difference column is the smallest difference detected with the stated power; it must be no larger than the difference you need.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For each n, the Difference column is the **smallest difference** detected with 90% power. A larger n gives a smaller detectable difference."},{title:"Apply",body:"A detectable difference of 0.70 or less is needed: n = 20 gives 0.76 (too big); n = 30 gives 0.61 (enough)."},{title:"Decide",body:"The smallest n in the table that works is 30."}],final:"n = 30 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["With n = 15 only differences of 0.90 SD or more are detected with 90% power; 0.7 SD is smaller.","With n = 20 the detectable difference is 0.76 SD, still larger than 0.7 SD, so the power for 0.7 SD is below 90%.","","n = 40 also works, but it is not the smallest size in the table that does."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–285, 290"},{id:"sv-3-265",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`Four planned studies compare two means. Which one will need the **largest** sample size (same α and power)?

| Study | Difference to detect (δ) | Process SD (S) |
|---|---|---|
| S1 | 2.0 min | 1.0 min |
| S2 | 1.5 min | 3.0 min |
| S3 | 4.0 min | 4.0 min |
| S4 | 0.9 min | 1.2 min |`,options:["S1","S2","S3","S4"],answer:1,hint:"Detectability depends on $\\dfrac{\\delta}{S}$: the smaller the ratio, the larger the sample.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: sample size depends on **delta and sigma together**, through the ratio $\\dfrac{\\delta}{S}$."},{title:"Compute the ratios",body:"$$\\text{S1}=\\dfrac{2.0}{1.0}=2.0\\qquad \\text{S2}=\\dfrac{1.5}{3.0}=0.5\\qquad \\text{S3}=\\dfrac{4.0}{4.0}=1.0\\qquad \\text{S4}=\\dfrac{0.9}{1.2}=0.75$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"The smallest ratio (S2 = 0.5) needs the largest sample."}],final:"S2 (ratio 0.5) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["S1 has the largest δ-to-S ratio (2.0), so it needs the smallest sample.","","S3 has the largest SD, but its δ is also large (ratio 1.0).","S4 has the smallest δ, but its SD is small too (ratio 0.75, larger than 0.5)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–285, 290"},{id:"sv-3-266",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"When should a project team agree on the size of difference that would be **practically significant**?",options:["After the test, by looking at whichever difference turned out to be significant","Only if the p-value is below α","Before the data are collected (in Measure), so the statistical test and the sample size can be set up to detect that difference","In Control, when the savings are verified"],answer:2,hint:"Decide the practical difference first, then size the sample so the statistical test can see it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: in the Measure phase the team should agree what practical difference matters, and the **statistical difference tested must match it**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Agree δ first, then use it to set the sample size and to judge the result."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Deciding it afterwards, or only when p < α, defeats the purpose."}],final:"Agree δ before collecting data → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Choosing the 'important' difference after seeing the result invites bias and gives no basis for the sample size.","The practical threshold is needed whatever the p-value, and above all to size the study beforehand.","","By Control the decision has already been made; the threshold must guide the analysis."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–285"},{id:"sv-3-267",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After a new diagnostic tool was introduced, the **first-time-fix rate** rose from **78% to 86%** (p = 0.002, α = 0.05). The business case said a gain of **5 percentage points** would pay for the tool. What is the BEST conclusion?",options:["The gain is statistically significant but not practically significant","The gain is practically significant but not statistically significant","Neither, because a p-value of 0.002 is too small to be trusted","The gain is both statistically and practically significant, so the change is worth acting on"],answer:3,hint:"Statistical: p < α. Practical: the difference is at least the size that pays.",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.002 < 0.05 → statistically significant."},{title:"Practical",body:"Gain = 86 − 78 = 8 points ≥ 5 points → practically significant."},{title:"Decide",body:"Both → real and worth it: act on it."}],final:"Both significant → answer **D**",whyWrong:["An 8-point gain is larger than the 5-point threshold, so it is practically significant.","p = 0.002 < 0.05, so it is statistically significant.","A small p-value is strong evidence against H₀, not a reason for distrust.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–285"},{id:"sv-3-268",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team is testing whether a coaching programme changes **agent schedule adherence**, a transactional process driven by human behaviour. They must choose α before the test. Which value does the book suggest as typical for this kind of project?",options:["α = 0.01","α = 0.05","α = 0.10","α = 0.50"],answer:2,hint:"α: 0.05 most common; 0.10 transactional or human behaviour; 0.01 rare.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's α guide: **0.05** most common (manufacturing), **0.10** for transactional projects involving human behaviour, **0.01** only when H₀ must be very hard to reject."},{title:"Apply",body:"Adherence depends on human behaviour → α = 0.10, set before the test."}],final:"α = 0.10 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["0.01 is used rarely, only when H₀ must be very hard to reject.","0.05 is the most common default (typical in manufacturing), but the book suggests 0.10 for transactional, human-behaviour projects.","","A 50% false-alarm risk is far too high for any decision."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–288"},{id:"sv-3-269",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"After a process change, mean **provisioning time** is almost unchanged (2-sample t-test p = 0.62), but the standard deviation appears to have fallen from **3.1 h to 1.4 h**. The team is about to report 'no significant improvement'. What should the Black Belt advise?",options:["Test the variances as well; a significant and practically important improvement can be in the spread, not only in the mean","Report no improvement, because the t-test is the test that decides whether a change worked","Report that the means are now proven equal, so the change had no effect","Repeat the t-test with a larger sample until it becomes significant"],answer:0,hint:"The difference that matters can be in the mean or in the variance: test the right parameter.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the practical difference can be in the **mean or in the variance**. Six Sigma aims to move the mean and/or **reduce the standard deviation**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Run a test for equal variances (F-test or Levene, depending on normality) and judge whether the smaller spread is practically valuable."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"A t-test cannot detect a change in spread, and 'proven equal' is the wrong wording."}],final:"Test the variances → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A t-test only compares means; it cannot see a reduction in variation.","p = 0.62 means fail to reject equal means; it proves nothing, and it says nothing about the spread.","Chasing significance on the means ignores where the real change is, and the means may truly be the same."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–285"},{id:"sv-3-270",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement correctly defines **β (beta)**?",options:["The probability of rejecting H₀ when H₀ is true","The probability of failing to reject H₀ when H₀ is false","The probability of rejecting H₀ when H₀ is false","The probability of failing to reject H₀ when H₀ is true"],answer:1,hint:"β = missing a real difference (failing to reject a false H₀); power = 1 − β.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"β is the Type II error risk: saying 'no difference' (fail to reject H₀) when a real difference exists (H₀ false)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Rejecting a true H₀ is α; rejecting a false H₀ is power; failing to reject a true H₀ is the correct decision, with probability 1 − α."}],final:"Fail to reject a false H₀ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is α, the Type I error risk.","","This is the power of the test (1 − β).","This is a correct decision, with probability 1 − α (the confidence level)."],trap:"All four options use the same words; only the pairing of decision and truth changes.",bookRef:"Book pp. 288–289"},{id:"sv-3-271",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In hypothesis testing, the quantity **1 − α** is called:",options:["The power of the test","The β risk","The confidence level","The p-value"],answer:2,hint:"1 − α = confidence (correctly not rejecting a true H₀); 1 − β = power.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"1 − α is the probability of correctly not rejecting a true H₀: the **confidence level** (95% when α = 0.05)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Power is 1 − β; β and p are different quantities."}],final:"Confidence level → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Power is 1 − β, the probability of detecting a real difference.","β is the Type II error risk; it is not defined as 1 − α.","","The p-value is calculated from the data; α and 1 − α are set in advance."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-272",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Instead of one ANOVA, an analyst runs **4** separate 2-sample t-tests (each new vendor against the incumbent), each at α = 0.05. If none of the vendors really differs, what is the probability of **at least one** false alarm?",options:["0.050","0.185","0.200","0.815"],answer:1,hint:"Overall Type I risk for k independent tests: $1-(1-\\alpha)^{k}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\alpha=0.05$ per test, $k=4$ tests."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\alpha_{\\text{overall}}=1-(1-\\alpha)^{k}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$(1-0.05)^{4}=0.95\\times 0.95\\times 0.95\\times 0.95=0.8145$$"},{title:"Finish",body:"$$\\alpha_{\\text{overall}}=1-0.8145=0.185$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About an 18.5% chance of at least one false 'significant' result, which is why the book recommends ANOVA for three or more means."}],final:"≈ 0.185 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Assumed the overall risk stays at the per-test α; it grows with every extra test.","","Added the four α values (4 × 0.05); the risks combine as 1 − (1 − α) to the power k, not by addition.","Calculated the chance of no false alarm, (1 − α) to the power k, and forgot to subtract it from 1."],bookRef:"Book pp. 291–292"},{id:"sv-3-273",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A pilot of a new fault-diagnosis method gave p = 0.31, and the team concluded the method does not cut **repair time**. Later they find that the repair-time logging system has a **%Study Var of 45%**. How does this finding affect the pilot's conclusion?",options:["It raises α, so the pilot's result is probably a false alarm","It has no effect, because hypothesis tests already allow for measurement error","It proves that the new method really has no effect","Imprecise measurements inflate the variation in the data and raise β, so a real improvement may have been missed"],answer:3,hint:"β (a missed improvement) rises when the sample is too small or the data are imprecise or inaccurate.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's likely reasons for a β error: the **sample size was too small**, or the data were **imprecise or inaccurate**."},{title:"Apply",body:"%Study Var 45% is unacceptable (> 30%); the extra noise widens the spread, cuts power and raises β. Fix the measurement system and test again."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"No rejection was made, so α is irrelevant, and nothing has been proven."}],final:"Noisy measurement → higher β → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A false alarm is a rejection of H₀; the pilot did not reject H₀, so α is not the issue.","Measurement error adds to the observed spread; the test cannot separate it from process variation.","Failing to reject proves nothing, and a poor gauge makes the 'no effect' result even less trustworthy.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-274",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A sponsor wants **fewer false alarms** (wrongly concluding that a change works) **and fewer missed improvements** in the team's hypothesis tests. Which single action reduces both risks?",options:["Increase the sample size","Lower α from 0.05 to 0.01","Raise α from 0.05 to 0.10","Switch from a two-tailed to a one-tailed test"],answer:0,hint:"For a fixed n, α and β trade off; only a larger n (or less noisy data) lowers both.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a fixed sample size, α and β trade off: making H₀ harder to reject makes it easier to miss a real difference."},{title:"Apply",body:"A larger sample shrinks the standard error, so the test can keep α low and still have high power (low β)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Changing α moves risk from one type to the other; the tail choice depends on the question."}],final:"Larger sample size → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Lowering α cuts false alarms but, for a fixed n, raises β (more missed improvements).","Raising α cuts β but increases false alarms.","A one-tailed test gains power in one direction only and must follow from the question, not be chosen to cut risk."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–292"},{id:"sv-3-275",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Before a pilot, the team checks the power of a 2-sample t-test:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
2-Sample t Test
Testing mean 1 = mean 2 (versus ≠)
α = 0.05   Assumed standard deviation = 4

Difference   Sample Size   Power
     3           12        0.42

The sample size is for each group.
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["β = 0.42: the risk of missing a real difference is acceptable","β = 0.58: the pilot would miss a real 3-unit difference more often than it detects it, so the sample size should be increased","α = 0.58: the pilot would give too many false alarms","The power is 0.42, so there is a 42% chance that H₀ is true"],answer:1,hint:"Power = 1 − β; the book's usual target is β = 0.10 (power 0.90).",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Difference 3, n = 12 per group, Power = 0.42."},{title:"Compute β",body:"$$\\beta=1-\\text{Power}=1-0.42=0.58$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"β = 0.58 is far above the usual 0.10, so the pilot is more likely to miss a real 3-unit difference than to find it. Increase n (enter power 0.90 and solve for the sample size)."}],final:"β = 0.58, increase n → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.42 is the power, not β; β = 1 − 0.42 = 0.58, far above the book's usual 0.10.","","α is fixed at 0.05 in the output; 0.58 is β, the chance of missing a real difference.","Power is the probability of detecting a real difference of 3 units, not a probability about H₀."],bookRef:"Book pp. 291–292"},{id:"sv-3-276",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab's sample-size calculation for a planned comparison of two vendors:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
2-Sample t Test
Testing mean 1 = mean 2 (versus ≠)
α = 0.05   Assumed standard deviation = 2.2

Difference   Sample Size   Target Power   Actual Power
     2           27            0.9          0.906222

The sample size is for each group.
\`\`\`

How many tickets must be collected **in total**?`,options:["14","27","52","54"],answer:3,hint:"For a 2-sample t-test, Minitab's sample size is per group.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Sample Size = 27, and the note says 'for each group'."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$n_{\\text{total}}=27\\times 2=54$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Collect 27 tickets from each vendor (54 in all) for 90% power to detect a 2-unit difference."}],final:"54 in total → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Split 27 between the two vendors (about 14 each); 27 is already per group.","Read 27 as the total, but the note says the sample size is for each group.","Used the degrees of freedom (2n − 2) instead of the number of tickets.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 291–292"},{id:"sv-3-277",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Each of the following describes a **Type II error** EXCEPT:",options:["Failing to recognise an improvement that really happened","Concluding that two processes are the same when they really differ","The producer's risk of implementing a change that does not really work","The consumer's risk, with probability β"],answer:2,hint:"Type II = β = consumer's risk = missing a real difference; Type I = α = producer's risk = false alarm.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Type II (β, consumer's risk): failing to reject a false H₀, saying 'no difference' when there is one."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Implementing a change that does not work is the **producer's risk**, a Type I error (α)."}],final:"Producer's risk is Type I → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Missing a real improvement is the book's description of β.","Saying 'same' when they differ is exactly a Type II error.","","The book calls β the consumer's risk."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–289"},{id:"sv-3-278",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the book, α is usually set at 0.05 while β is allowed to be 0.10. What reason does the book give for making α the smaller risk?",options:["α is easier to calculate than β, so it can be made smaller","Overlooking a real X is more costly than acting on a false one","β cannot be controlled by the team, so only α can be set","Teams are more cautious about wrongly claiming that an X matters, and spending money on it, than about overlooking an X"],answer:3,hint:"α = acting on a false X (spending money); β = overlooking an X.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: we are more careful about wrongly **claiming** an X matters (and spending money on it) than about **overlooking** an X."},{title:"Apply",body:"So the false-alarm risk α is held lower (0.05) than the miss risk β (0.10, power 0.90)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"β is controlled through n and power; the reversed option contradicts the book."}],final:"Caution about false claims → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Both are set as planning choices; ease of calculation is not the reason.","This reverses the book's reasoning, which treats a false claim (α) as the more serious risk.","β is controlled through the sample size and the power chosen in planning.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-279",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"At the book's step 'select the test and the risk levels', which pair of risk levels is usually chosen?",options:["α = 0.05 and β = 0.10 (power 0.90)","α = 0.10 and β = 0.05 (power 0.95)","α = 0.05 and β = 0.05 (power 0.95)","α = 0.05 and power = 0.10"],answer:0,hint:"Book defaults: α = 0.05, β = 0.10, power = 1 − β.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's six steps use **α = 0.05 and β = 0.10** by default, so power = 1 − 0.10 = 0.90."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Swapping α and β, or writing power = 0.10, are wording traps."}],final:"α = 0.05, β = 0.10 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Swapped α and β; the book keeps α smaller than β.","β = 0.05 is not the book's default; the usual value is 0.10.","Confuses power with β: a power of 0.10 would miss real differences 90% of the time."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–289"},{id:"sv-3-280",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team wants to know whether the median **porting time** differs from the **24-hour** target. They have **11** orders; the data are clearly **asymmetric** and fail the normality test. Which test is MOST appropriate?",options:["1-sample Wilcoxon signed-rank test","1-sample sign test","1-sample t-test","Mann-Whitney test"],answer:1,hint:"One median vs a target: sign test (no symmetry needed) or Wilcoxon (symmetric data).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"One sample, median vs a target, non-normal data → **1-sample sign** or **1-sample Wilcoxon**. Wilcoxon needs roughly symmetric data."},{title:"Apply",body:"Small, asymmetric sample → sign test."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Wilcoxon (symmetry), t (normality), Mann-Whitney (two samples)."}],final:"1-sample sign test → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The Wilcoxon test assumes a roughly symmetric distribution; with clearly asymmetric data the sign test is the safer choice.","","A t-test on 11 non-normal values compares a mean and relies on normality.","Mann-Whitney compares two independent samples, not one sample against a target."],bookRef:"Book pp. 370–377"},{id:"sv-3-281",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Median **activation time** is to be compared across **four** regions, each with its own independent set of orders. Two regions fail the normality test; the box plots show skew but **no extreme outliers**. Which test is MOST appropriate?",options:["Mood's median test","One-way ANOVA","Kruskal-Wallis test","Friedman test"],answer:2,hint:"Three or more independent medians: Kruskal-Wallis; with many outliers: Mood's median; blocked: Friedman.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Three or more independent groups with non-normal data → compare **medians** with Kruskal-Wallis (Mood's median if outliers abound)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Four independent regions, skewed, no extreme outliers → Kruskal-Wallis."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"ANOVA (normality), Mood's (robust to outliers but less powerful), Friedman (blocks)."}],final:"Kruskal-Wallis → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Mood's median is preferred when outliers dominate; without extreme outliers Kruskal-Wallis is more powerful.","ANOVA compares means and assumes normal data in every group.","","Friedman is for a blocked design (the same blocks under each treatment), not for independent groups."],bookRef:"Book pp. 370–377"},{id:"sv-3-282",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The **same 6 agents** each handle a batch of tickets with **three** different troubleshooting scripts, and the median handling time is recorded for every agent–script combination. Agent skill varies a lot and the data are not normal. Which test compares the three scripts while allowing for the agent effect?",options:["Kruskal-Wallis test","Mood's median test","Mann-Whitney test","Friedman test"],answer:3,hint:"Blocked design (each block sees every treatment) with non-normal data → Friedman.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each agent is a **block** that sees every treatment (script). For non-normal data in a blocked design the test is **Friedman**, the non-parametric counterpart of a two-way ANOVA without replication."},{title:"Apply",body:"6 agents × 3 scripts → Friedman, with agent as the block."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Kruskal-Wallis and Mood's ignore the blocks; Mann-Whitney handles two groups only."}],final:"Friedman → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Kruskal-Wallis treats the groups as independent and ignores the agent blocks, so agent-to-agent variation swamps the script effect.","Mood's median also assumes independent groups and ignores the blocking by agent.","Mann-Whitney compares only two independent samples.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.3.4; Book pp. 370–372"},{id:"sv-3-283",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Two server clusters both produce **normally distributed** response times. The team wants to know whether one cluster is **more consistent** (less spread) than the other. Which test is MOST appropriate?",options:["F-test for two variances","Bartlett's test","Levene's test","2-sample t-test"],answer:0,hint:"Variances: 2 normal → F; more than 2 normal → Bartlett; any non-normal → Levene.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's variance summary: **2 normal samples → F-test**; more than 2 normal → Bartlett; any non-normal → Levene."},{title:"Apply",body:"Two clusters, normal data, a question about spread → F-test."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Bartlett (more than 2 groups), Levene (non-normal), t-test (means)."}],final:"F-test → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Bartlett's test is the roadmap choice for more than two normal samples.","Levene's test is the choice when the data are not normal.","A 2-sample t-test compares means, not spread."],bookRef:"Book pp. 340–349"},{id:"sv-3-284",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Of **400** calls sampled this month, **332** were resolved on first contact. The team wants to test whether the **first-contact resolution rate** differs from the **85%** target. Which test fits?",options:["2-proportion test","1-proportion test","1-sample t-test","Mann-Whitney test"],answer:1,hint:"Attribute (yes/no) data, one sample vs a target proportion → 1-proportion test.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Attribute data are non-normal by definition. One proportion vs a target → **1-proportion test**, with H₀: p = 0.85."},{title:"Apply",body:"332 of 400 resolved (83%) vs the 85% target → 1-proportion test."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The 2-proportion test needs two samples; t and Mann-Whitney need continuous data."}],final:"1-proportion test → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A 2-proportion test compares two samples; here one sample is compared with a target.","","The data are counts of resolved or not resolved (attribute), not a continuous measurement.","Mann-Whitney compares the medians of two continuous samples."],bookRef:"Book pp. 372, 396"},{id:"sv-3-285",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A design rule says the standard deviation of **jitter** must not exceed **2 ms**. A sample of 30 normally distributed readings is available. Which test checks the process spread against this target?",options:["F-test for two variances","1-sample t-test","1-sample variance (chi-square) test","Levene's test"],answer:2,hint:"One variance vs a target value (normal data) → 1-sample variance test (chi-square).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For normal data, one standard deviation or variance against a target → **1-sample variance test** using the chi-square distribution, with H₀: σ² = σ₀²."},{title:"Apply",body:"One sample of jitter vs a 2 ms limit → 1-sample variance test."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"F and Levene compare samples with each other; t tests a mean."}],final:"1-sample variance test → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The F-test compares the variances of two samples; here one sample is compared with a target.","The 1-sample t-test compares a mean with a target, not a standard deviation.","","Levene's test compares the variances of two or more samples with non-normal data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 340–349"},{id:"sv-3-286",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team plans to compare the centre of **download speed** for two independent broadband technologies. Normality tests give:

\`\`\`
Probability Plot of Speed by Technology
Technology   N    AD      P-Value
FTTH         25   0.36    0.421
FWA          25   1.42   <0.005
\`\`\`

Which test should be used to compare the two technologies?`,options:["2-sample t-test, because the FTTH sample is normal","Paired t-test, because both samples have 25 values","Kruskal-Wallis test, because the data are continuous and non-normal","Mann-Whitney test, because one of the two samples is not normal"],answer:3,hint:"If just one distribution is non-normal, use the non-parametric test; two independent medians → Mann-Whitney.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"FTTH p = 0.421 (fail to reject normality); FWA p < 0.005 (not normal)."},{title:"Decide",body:"The book: if **just one** distribution is not normal, use a non-parametric test. Two independent groups → **Mann-Whitney**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The 2-sample t needs both normal; equal n is not pairing; Kruskal-Wallis is for 3 or more groups."}],final:"Mann-Whitney → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Both samples must be normal for a 2-sample t-test; the book says that if just one is not normal, use a non-parametric test.","Equal sample sizes do not make data paired; the two technologies serve different lines.","Kruskal-Wallis is the roadmap choice for three or more independent groups; for two groups the roadmap test is Mann-Whitney.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 370–377"},{id:"sv-3-287",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Before comparing mean **ticket backlog** across three teams, an analyst checks for equal variances. All three samples passed the normality test (AD p-values 0.38, 0.52 and 0.27).

\`\`\`
Test for Equal Variances: Backlog vs Team
Method      Test Statistic   P-Value
Bartlett         6.97         0.031
Levene           2.81         0.068
\`\`\`

At α = 0.05, which reading is correct?`,options:["Use Bartlett's result because the data are normal: p = 0.031, so the variances are not all equal","Use Levene's result because it is always the safer test: p = 0.068, so the variances are equal","Use Bartlett's result: p = 0.031, so fail to reject equal variances","Use whichever p-value is larger, so that the ANOVA assumptions are met"],answer:0,hint:"Equal-variance test: normal data → Bartlett (F for two groups); non-normal → Levene. Reject when p < α.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For more than two **normal** samples the book uses **Bartlett's test**; Levene's test is for non-normal data."},{title:"Apply",body:"All AD p-values are above 0.05 → read Bartlett: p = 0.031 < 0.05 → reject H₀: at least one variance differs."},{title:"Next",body:"Unequal variances must be taken into account when the test of means is chosen."}],final:"Bartlett, p = 0.031 → variances differ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Levene is the choice for non-normal data; here all samples are normal, and 'the variances are equal' is also the 'accept H₀' wording trap.","p = 0.031 < 0.05 means reject H₀ (equal variances), not fail to reject.","Picking the p-value that gives the preferred answer is not a valid rule; the data's normality decides the test."],bookRef:"Book pp. 340–349"},{id:"sv-3-288",phase:3,topic:"3.3.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"A team counts **defective** orders (yes/no) in samples from different sales channels. All of the following tests could be appropriate for this kind of data EXCEPT:",options:["1-proportion test","Mann-Whitney test","2-proportion test","Chi-square test of independence"],answer:1,hint:"Attribute (proportion) data: 1-proportion, 2-proportion, chi-square. Mann-Whitney is for continuous medians.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defective yes/no is attribute data. Its roadmap: 1-proportion, 2-proportion and chi-square (contingency table)."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Mann-Whitney compares the medians of two continuous, non-normal samples."}],final:"Mann-Whitney is NOT for proportions → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Comparing one channel's defective rate with a target is a 1-proportion test.","","Comparing two channels' defective rates is a 2-proportion test.","Comparing defective rates across several channels uses a chi-square contingency table."],bookRef:"Book pp. 372, 396"}];export{e as items};
